/**
\class pdg::Camera
\ingroup Animation Graphics
\brief Animated view owned by a Port or shared by layers.

Scene-owned cameras advance on their scene clock. Layer overrides remain available;
\ref pdg::Scene describes ownership and pause behavior.

See \ref camera_output for camera visibility, composed opacity, fades and crossfades.

Camera inherits EventEmitter event handling and Animated movement, rotation, scale, reflection, pivots, helpers,
timed chains and schedule controls. Moving its location right moves visible world
content left. Rotation uses radians and is inverted in the view; zoom and scale
magnify content. Camera size is animation metadata and does not resize a viewport.
For a Port-owned camera, location + centerOffset maps to the Port anchor, in port coordinates. Anchors default to (0,0). An explicit layer camera maps its focus to the center of its stored viewport.

A layer's explicit camera overrides its Scene camera and port camera. Clearing
the explicit camera restores inheritance from its Scene, or from the current Port
for a legacy layer. Every Port owns its camera.
Attachments retain cameras; getCamera()/getEffectiveCamera() preserve shared
JavaScript identity. Attached cameras advance once per runtime tick regardless
of attachment count or whether a layer is paused or hidden. Scene-owned cameras
advance on scene time and stop when their scene pauses. animate(seconds)
additionally advances state, and is intended for manual/detached animation.

Direct Port Renderer drawing applies the camera once outside local Attributes
transforms, including nested Drawings. Layers project sprites, Parts, particles
and tiles through their effective camera once. Port clipping and clear operations
remain in port coordinates. MVC Views automatically draw in screen space.
For direct screen drawing, disable camera drawing temporarily and restore it in
finally. Port conversions still use the camera while drawing is disabled.

\note Port attachment and rendering require a graphics-capable build. Camera
animation and explicit layer attachments also work headlessly. Camera zoom must
stay finite and positive, and its view must have finite, nonzero scale.

\code{.js}
const camera = port.getCamera();
port.setCameraAnchor(new pdg.Point(320, 240));
const actors = pdg.createSpriteLayer(port);
const background = pdg.createSpriteLayer(port);
background.setCameraParallax(0.5, 0);
camera.setLocation(100, 200).zoomTo(2, 0.5, pdg.linearTween);
// Both layers inherit camera; background ignores zoom and scrolls half as far.
\endcode
*/

/**
\fn Camera pdg::Camera::setZoom(number zoom)
\brief Set view magnification immediately.
\param zoom Finite positive factor; 2 doubles apparent content size.
\return This camera for chaining.
Initial zoom is 1. Zoom multiplies Animated scale without changing viewport size.
Invalid zoom and immediate operations in a timed sequence throw.
*/

/**
\fn Camera pdg::Camera::zoomTo(number zoom, number seconds, number easing = easeInOutQuad)
\brief Animate view magnification.
\param zoom Finite positive target factor.
\param seconds Finite nonnegative duration in seconds; zero applies immediately.
\param easing Registered easing constant; defaults to easeInOutQuad.
\return This camera for chaining.
Participates in wait(), andThen(), cancellation and schedule pause/resume.
Emits eventType_ZoomComplete on this camera; subscribe with onZoomComplete().

\see onZoomComplete()
*/

/**
\fn Camera pdg::Camera::setPixelSnapping(boolean snap = true)
\brief Round final view translation without rounding persistent camera pose.
\param snap Enable snapping; initially false on a new camera.
\return This camera for chaining.
*/



/**
\fn void pdg::SpriteLayer::setCameraParallax(number movementRatio = 1, number zoomRatio = 1)
\brief Adjust camera scrolling and zoom for this layer.
\param movementRatio Finite factor multiplying camera location.
\param zoomRatio Finite exponent applied to camera zoom.
Zero ignores that component. Rotation, scale, reflection and pivot still apply.
The destination port's anchor remains the same for explicit and inherited cameras.
Layer bounds, fades, visibility, physics and simulation pause remain independent.
*/

/**
\fn void pdg::SpriteLayer::setWorldBounds(Rect bounds)
\brief Set world bounds for sprite boundary checks.
\param bounds Finite ordered rectangle independent of camera and viewport.
Tile-layer map dimensions initialize its bounds. Camera motion never changes them.
*/

/**
\fn Point pdg::Port::worldToPort(Point point)
\brief Project a world point using this port's camera and anchor.
\param point World-coordinate point.
\return Port-coordinate point; using the Port's built-in camera.
Use layerToPortPoint() for a layer's explicit camera or parallax settings.
*/

/**
\fn Point pdg::Port::portToWorld(Point point)
\brief Invert this port's camera view.
\param point Port-coordinate point.
\return World-coordinate point; using the Port's built-in camera.
Use portToLayerPoint() for a layer's explicit camera or parallax settings.
*/

/**
\fn Port pdg::Port::setCameraAnchor(Point anchor)
\brief Set the Port-owned camera's anchor within its full-port viewport.
\param anchor Point relative to drawing-area top-left; initially (0,0).
\return This port for chaining.
Each port can use a different anchor without changing the shared camera pose.
*/

/**
\fn Port pdg::Port::setCameraDrawingEnabled(boolean enabled)
\brief Select world or screen coordinates for direct drawing.
\param enabled True to apply the port camera; false for screen-space drawing.
\return This port for chaining.
Coordinate conversions and layer camera selection are unchanged by this setting.
\code{.js}
const previous = port.getCameraDrawingEnabled();
port.setCameraDrawingEnabled(false);
try { port.drawRect(new pdg.Rect(10, 10, 30, 30), hudAttributes); }
finally { port.setCameraDrawingEnabled(previous); }
\endcode
*/

/**
\fn Camera pdg::Camera::zoom(number factor, number seconds, number easing = easeInOutQuad)
\brief Animate magnification by a positive multiplier.
\param factor Finite positive multiplier. 2 doubles zoom; 0.5 halves it.
\param seconds Finite nonnegative duration in seconds.
\param easing Easing constant; defaults to easeInOutQuad.
\return This camera for chaining.

Queued multipliers use the zoom at the start of the operation. Emits
eventType_ZoomComplete when completed, just like zoomTo().
\code{.js}
camera.zoomTo(2, 0.5).andThen().zoom(0.5, 0.5); // ends at zoom 1
\endcode
*/

/**
\fn IEventHandler pdg::Camera::onZoomComplete(function callback)
\brief Listen for this camera's completed zoom operations.
\param callback Function called synchronously with a CameraZoomInfo event.
Its fields are camera, zoom (completed magnification), emitter (the camera), and
 eventType (eventType_ZoomComplete). Return false to allow other handlers
 to run, or true to mark the event handled.
\return Handler with cancel() to remove this subscription.

Both zoom() and zoomTo() emit once per completed operation. Zero-duration
operations emit synchronously unless queued or delayed; queued operations emit
when advanced. Cancelled or replaced operations and setZoom() do not emit.
A shared camera emits once, independently of its number of attachments. Schedule
pause delays completion. Restored scheduled operations emit on the restored
camera; event handlers must be registered again after loading.
Use camera.addHandler(handler, pdg.eventType_ZoomComplete) to listen with
an IEventHandler directly. Completion belongs to Camera, replacing the former
SpriteLayer action_ZoomComplete event and layer.onZoomComplete().
\code{.js}
const subscription = camera.onZoomComplete(function(event) {
    console.log('Zoom completed at', event.zoom);
    return false;
});
camera.zoomTo(2, 0.5);
// Later: subscription.cancel();
\endcode
*/
